 75CU2c  (2016)                                                                 
 75CU c  Compiled by G. G~urdal (Millsaps College), October 1, 2016             
 75CU c  {+75}Cu was produced via fragmentation of  {+86}Kr primary beam on a   
 75CU2c  rotating 755-|mm-thick Be target at 60.4 MeV/nucleon at the Grand      
 75CU3c  Accelerateur d'Ions Lourds (GANIL) facility. The reaction products     
 75CU4c  were selected and separated in flight by the LISE2k spectrometer. Two  
 75CU5c  consecutive silicon detectors located at the focal plane of LISE2k     
 75CU6c  were used to detect the heavy ions. The identified fragments were      
 75CU7c  implanted in a rectangular foil. The |g-rays were detected using HPGe  
 75CU8c  detectors placed on both side of the implantation foil. A LEPs         
 75CU9c  detector was used to detect low-energy |g-rays. The total |g-ray       
 75CUac  detection efficiency was measured as 20% at 130 keV. The time elapsed  
 75CUbc  between the implantation of an ion and a delayed |g-ray  was measured  
 75CUcc  using TACs with 4|ms range. Measured: |DE, magnetic rigidity, tof,     
 75CUdc  E|g, I|g, |g-|g coin, isomer T{-1/2}. Comparison with Shell Model      
 75CUec  calculations.                                                          
 75CU cL E$From E|g data.                                                       
 75CU cL J$The J|p of excited states were discussed and many scenarios with     
 75CU2cL comparisons of transition strengths with shell model calculations      
 75CU3cL are given in 2016Pe14, however, no definite conclusion could be        
 75CU4cL reached. 2016Pe14 favor the scenario of (1/2-) for the 61.7-keV        
 75CU5cL level and (3/2-) for the 66.2-keV level based on the fact that         
 75CU6cL the upper isomer is almost exclusively populated directly in           
 75CU7cL the reaction.                                                          
